data: {"id":"evt_0eee251dc001XCnw2s6s0NuqxJ","type":"message.part.updated","properties":{"sessionID":"ses_f111daf97ffeEpTXXz2p2WARoR","part":{"type":"text","text":"The task is the issue's text between the lines <<<ISSUE-5356e5ac505eda39 and ISSUE-5356e5ac505eda39>>>.\n<<<ISSUE-5356e5ac505eda39\nWrite one.txt and two.txt.\nISSUE-5356e5ac505eda39>>>\n\nHow to work: reproduce the reported fault before you change code. When the issue was filed from a SigNoz alert, open your last message with a ```repro block holding the shell command that reproduced it: the factory runs it at the base, where it must fail, and on your change, where it must pass, so it must bring its own test (for example a here-document that writes the test file) rather than name a test your change adds. If you fix a fault you could not reproduce exactly, put a line \"not_reproduced: <the fault you found and why the change fixes it>\" in your last message. If you cannot reproduce it and find no fault, call request_info with what you tried and what you need instead of guessing. If only a human can decide, call escalate_to_human with your recommendation. These two tools are your only way to ask: nobody answers a question in a message. You have no credentials and cannot push, by design: when you end, the factory commits your working tree, pushes it and opens the pull request; do not commit, push or look for a token. The sandbox has no C compiler and no database: run go test without -race and skip the tests that need one; the pull request checks run both. No server, no SSH, no network beyond the module proxy. End with a short last message: what you changed, and whether you recommend merging it and why.","messageID":"msg_0eee25082001LsqP6i8x36x99z","sessionID":"ses_f111daf97ffeEpTXXz2p2WARoR","id":"prt_0eee25087001SBoTB0WctUgXnQ"},"time":1790714204636}}

